          "diff_hunk": "READ\", ImmutableMap.of(\"bucket\", \"b1\"), \"op-1\", durationMetric);\n+    Operation second =\n+        createOperation(\"READ\", ImmutableMap.of(\"bucket\", \"b1\"), \"op-1\", durationMetric);\n+    ImmutableSet<Object> operations = ImmutableSet.of(first);\n+\n+    assertThat(first).isEqualTo(second);\n+    assertThat(first.hashCode()).isEqualTo(second.hashCode());\n+    assertThat(operations).contains(first);\n+  }",
          "body": "![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)\n\nThe assertion `assertThat(operations).contains(first)` is trivial because `operations` was initialized with `first` (`ImmutableSet.of(first)`). To properly test the `equals` and `hashCode` contract in a hash-based collection, you should assert that the set contains `second` (which is a different instance with identical fields).\n\n```java\n    assertThat(operations).contains(second);\n```",
